from pathlib import Path
import json
p=Path('content/networks/03-tcp/tcp-flow-control.questions.json');q=json.loads(p.read_text())
q[0]['prompt']+=' Use the steady-state window/RTT payload model, with the full receive window available and no tighter congestion or application limit.'
q[0]['options'][3]['explanation']='3.3 Gbit/s does not follow from the stated window/RTT model and also exceeds the supplied 1 Gbit/s link capacity.'
q[1]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct. The advertised receive window constrains the sequence range of new data the receiver is willing to accept. It reflects buffer and update policy, rather than exactly equalling unused memory.'
q[1]['options'][3]['explanation']='Window and sequence checks can reject some injected segments, but they are not cryptographic authentication. The purpose of rwnd is receiver flow control.'
q[2]['prompt']='A capture shows repeated zero receive windows during an upload. Server instrumentation also shows a full receive queue and its receiving thread blocked on disk I/O instead of reading. Which explanation matches both observations?'
q[2]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. Here the capture and server instrumentation together establish that the blocked reader is not draining the queue. A zero window alone would not identify this unique cause.'
q[2]['options'][1]['explanation']='Network congestion may coexist, but it does not explain the independently observed blocked receiving thread and full queue. Zero windows specifically advertise receiver-side flow-control restriction.'
q[2]['workedExample']='1. The capture establishes receiver-side flow-control restriction.\n2. Server instrumentation supplies the cause in this scenario: the thread is blocked and the receive queue is full.\n3. Persist probes can obtain later window updates, but do not fix the blocked application.\n4. Investigate the disk/reader pipeline; do not infer this diagnosis from a zero window alone in every deployment.'
q[3]['prompt']+=' Treat 10 Gbit/s as the target payload rate, ignore protocol overhead, and assume buffer and congestion-window capacity can be provisioned.'
q[3]['workedExample']=q[3]['workedExample'].replace("You'd also need receive and send buffers of around 100 MB, well above Linux's default 6 MB maximum.","This requires roughly 100 MB of usable in-flight payload capacity, with enough implementation-specific buffer memory and congestion window. There is no universal Linux 6 MB maximum.")
q[4]['prompt']='On an established connection, a trace confirms a short header leaves immediately, its ACK is delayed by the server for 40 ms, and a separately written sub-MSS body remains in the client’s TCP send queue until that ACK arrives. Nagle is enabled, both windows are open and the server awaits the full request. Which mechanism explains this trace?'
q[4]['options'][0]['explanation']='The prompt says both advertised and congestion windows allow sending, so a zero window is not this trace’s cause.'
q[4]['options'][1]['explanation']='The stated trace identifies waiting for the header ACK with capacity available, rather than a congestion-window limit. Response duration alone would not establish that distinction.'
q[4]['options'][3]['explanation']='This is an established connection, so the listener’s SYN backlog does not explain the observed body hold.'
q[4]['workedExample']=q[4]['workedExample'].replace('After about 40 ms the Linux delayed-ACK timer fires','In the stated measured trace, after 40 ms the delayed-ACK timer fires').replace('Every request pays ~40 ms on a 0.3 ms network. Fix:','This confirmed interaction adds a wait; it is not a universal timer or outcome for every request. Possible remedies:')
q[5]['options'][0]['text']='Under the simplified classic rule, a short new-data send waits while data is outstanding unless a full segment can be formed'
q[5]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. This scopes the teaching rule to ordinary new-data sending; TCP variants and special control/retransmission paths require more detail.'
q[5]['options'][1]['text']='It must delay every full-sized ready segment in a bulk file transfer'
q[5]['options'][1]['explanation']='False. Nagle’s small-segment condition does not hold a full-sized ready segment. Partial tails and incremental small writes during a file transfer can still be affected.'
q[5]['options'][3]['explanation']='Correct. TCP_NODELAY disables Nagle’s small-segment waiting condition. Other limits and buffering can still delay transmission.'
q[5]['workedExample']='The stated classic rule buffers short new-data sends while earlier data remains outstanding, unless enough data forms a full segment. It was motivated by inefficient small interactive packets. TCP_NODELAY disables this condition per socket; receiver ACK scheduling is separate. This does not promise immediate wire transmission or no delays anywhere in a bulk transfer.'
q[6]['prompt']='A Linux backup receiver explicitly sets SO_RCVBUF, disabling receive autotuning. Captures then show an effective advertised window near 65,536 bytes; RTT is 120 ms and no other bottleneck is observed. Why can throughput be limited to roughly 4.4 Mbit/s?'
q[6]['options'][0]['text']='The explicit buffer policy prevents automatic growth, and the measured usable window gives a window/RTT ceiling near 4.4 Mbit/s'
q[6]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. Use the measured advertised window for this arithmetic. Linux doubles SO_RCVBUF requests for accounting; the request value alone does not establish the payload window.'
q[6]['options'][1]['explanation']='The evidence here identifies a receive-window limit. Buffer policy can affect overall traffic dynamics, so this is not a universal claim that buffers never influence loss.'
q[6]['workedExample']='1. An explicit SO_RCVBUF setting disables Linux receive autotuning for that socket.\n2. The prompt separately supplies the measured usable window: 65,536 bytes.\n3. 65,536 / 0.12 × 8 ≈ 4.369 Mbit/s under the steady-state model.\n4. Linux’s doubled accounting and overhead mean SO_RCVBUF itself is not the correct direct window measurement.\n5. Reconsider the explicit limit or allow autotuning, then measure again; sufficient memory, scaling and congestion window are also needed.'
q[7]['prompt']+=' Assume queued data still needs sending and a later probe/reply exchange is successfully delivered.'
q[7]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct under those delivery assumptions. A zero-window probe elicits a current-window acknowledgement; probes cannot guarantee progress through permanent loss or endpoint failure.'
q[7]['options'][2]['explanation']='Keepalive is a separate mechanism with configurable policy and timing. It is not a universal two-second replacement for zero-window probing.'
q[7]['options'][3]['explanation']='TCP includes zero-window probing to recover a lost window update. Applications can still observe blocking/timeouts if the window remains closed or probes cannot complete.'
q[7]['workedExample']='1. A zero advertised window prevents ordinary new-data sending while data remains queued.\n2. The pure window-update ACK is not tracked for ordinary data retransmission.\n3. Persist/zero-window probing solicits a fresh advertisement.\n4. Under the question’s successful delivery assumption, the reply reveals the reopened window and permits progress.\n5. Probe scheduling/backoff and failure policy depend on the TCP implementation; zero-window probing is distinct from idle keepalive.'
q[8]['prompt']='A download plateaus near 5.2 Mbit/s at 100 ms RTT. Captures on both sides of a newly installed firewall confirm the peers offer window scaling but the option is removed in transit, leaving unscaled operation. Why is that rate plausible in the steady-state window/RTT model?'
q[8]['options'][0]['explanation']='The rate alone would not exclude congestion or other limits. Here the captures separately establish the lack of window scaling.'
q[8]['options'][1]['explanation']='CPU limits can produce stable plateaus too. The supplied handshake evidence, rather than a rate pattern alone, identifies this scenario’s window constraint.'
q[8]['options'][3]['explanation']='Correct. The captures establish unscaled operation, and 65,535 / 0.1 × 8 ≈ 5.243 Mbit/s matches the model. Throughput alone would not prove option stripping.'
q[8]['workedExample']='1. Unscaled windows are at most 65,535 bytes.\n2. 65,535 / 0.1 × 8 = 5,242,800 bit/s.\n3. Endpoint and on-path captures establish that scaling was removed; the numerical match is supporting evidence, not a unique diagnosis.\n4. Inspect both handshake directions because inconsistent option rewriting can also produce disagreement or packet drops, not merely a clean throughput cap.'
q[9]['prompt']='A client sends a 200-byte header and 300-byte body in separate calls and a trace confirms the Nagle/delayed-ACK interaction. Which client changes can remove that specific dependency? For batching choices assume an empty send queue, both pieces supplied before packetisation, a 500-byte message below MSS and sufficient windows/buffer space. Select all that apply.'
q[9]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct under the batching assumptions: supplying the complete message avoids waiting for the first partial message’s ACK before supplying its remainder. sendall still does not guarantee one packet in general.'
q[9]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct under the stated assumptions: both buffers are supplied together. sendmsg may return a partial byte count, which the caller must handle; one call does not universally imply one packet.'
q[9]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct. This removes Nagle’s hold of the body. Other send constraints still apply, so it does not guarantee immediate transmission.'
q[9]['workedExample']='Batching can avoid the split-message dependency under the explicit assumptions; TCP_NODELAY removes Nagle’s waiting condition. Client-side QUICKACK changes the wrong endpoint’s ACK behaviour for this trace, and a larger send buffer does not override Nagle when capacity already exists. Neither batching nor NODELAY guarantees one write per packet or eliminates all possible transport delays.'
q[10]['prompt']+=' Use the simplified new-data budget, with consistent ACK/window origins, no recovery/probe exceptions, and sufficient queued data; ignore pacing and packetisation.'
q[10]['workedExample']=q[10]['workedExample'].replace("Here the **network** (cwnd) is the limit, not the receiver.","Here the sender’s congestion window is the tighter modelled constraint. That alone does not prove current network congestion, and the byte budget does not require immediate transmission.")
p.write_text(json.dumps(q,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
